# Lesson 4, batch 2: leading an honor at no-trump. QJT9 in dummy opposite A54; lead the queen, then the jack, then the ten, and East's king is trapped. Dummy's ♠A and ♥K are the entries.
title: Queen, jack, ten
id: L04-30
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.2, 3.5
student: S
board: 114
dealer: E
vulnerable: NS

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  A63    K64    QJT9   952
  E  JT852  53     K87    JT6
  S  K94    A82    A54    A873
  W  Q7     QJT97  632    KQ4

auction:
  E Pass
  S 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 15 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 3NT    = 10+ points: game
  E Pass
  S Pass
  W Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT and partner raised to game. You are declarer, because you
  bid no-trump first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  winners: 8
  spades: 2 = The ace and king.
  hearts: 2 = The ace and king.
  diamonds: 3 = Three once the king is gone. Four if East has the king and you lead the honors from dummy.
  clubs: 1 = The ace.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: Where can the ninth trick come from? Which hand should lead that suit, and how will you get there?
play:
  1   W HQ  N H4  E H3  S HA
      note: Eight obvious winners. The ninth can come from diamonds: if East has the king, lead the queen, jack and ten from dummy, and the king is trapped. Win in your hand, and keep dummy's ♥K and ♠A as ways across.
  2   S S4  W S7  N SA  E S2
      note: Over to dummy with the ♠A.
  3   N DQ  E D7  S D4*  W D2
      note: Lead the ♦Q from dummy.
      D4 key: Let the queen run. If East has the king, the queen wins and you're still in dummy.
      D4 not DA: Did you mean to win your own queen? Let it run: if East has the king, the queen wins and you can lead again from dummy.
  4   N DJ  E D8  S D5*  W D3
      note: The queen won. Now the jack, the same way.
      D5 key: Let it run again. East's king is still trapped.
      D5 not DA: Did you notice East may still have the king? Let the jack run.
  5   N DT  E DK  S DA  W D6
      note: Now the ten. East has only the king left and has to play it, and your ace wins. Dummy's nine is a winner.
  6   S H2  W HJ  N HK  E H5
      note: Over to dummy's ♥K to cash the nine.
  7   N D9  E S5  S C3  W H7
      note: The fourth diamond.
  8   N C2  E C6  S CA  W C4
  9   S SK  W SQ  N S3  E S8
      note: That's nine tricks.
  10  S H8  W HT  N H6  E ST
      note: Nothing left to win. Give up the rest.
  11  W H9  N C5  E SJ  S C7
  12  W CK  N C9  E CT  S C8
  13  W CQ  N S6  E CJ  S S9
      note: 9 tricks: 3NT made.

play recap:
  The plan: eight obvious winners, and a fourth diamond trick if East held
  the king and you led the diamonds from dummy.

  Leading the queen, then the jack, kept you in dummy each time they won.
  You could lead honors because you held the cards in between: the queen,
  jack, ten and nine together. When East had only the king left, it had to
  fall under your ace, and dummy's nine was good. You needed the ♥K to get
  back to it. If West had held the king, you would have made only three
  diamond tricks and gone down one.
